Original commit body from 36a6bb30c7795526dcaa63ccc4108840a81e270e:

`obj.bar()` has emitted `python:external:0-0:bar:unresolved` since WI-fuvuj.
`event.organizer.issued_gift_cards.create(...)` emitted NOTHING. Measured on
pretix: four calls to the same Django manager sink on adjacent lines, two
emitting and two silent.

ROOT CAUSE, LOCALIZED TO A LINE — which the filing said it was not. In py.py's
catch-all `elif isinstance(func, ast.Attribute)` branch, `_unwind_attribute_chain`
DOES return a chain for a local-rooted receiver (the root is an `ast.Name`), but
the emit is gated on `root_name in module_imports and len(chain_attrs) >= 2`. A
chain rooted at a local fails the first conjunct and falls through with NO
`else`. Depth was the only difference from the single-attribute form, and
nothing justified it.

The fix is that `else`, emitting `external` as the module: the receiver's type
is genuinely unknown, and synthesising `event.organizer.issued_gift_cards` as a
module path would assert a module that does not exist and could collide with a
catalogued entry of the same shape. Placed inside the module-import branch so
the class-rooted Django marker — which emits a module-qualified
`django.db.models` edge earlier — keeps winning; pinned by its own test.

IT COSTS TWICE. A function whose sink calls are all chain-shaped has no sink
edge, is never considered, and its flow is never reported — a false negative,
the expensive direction. The same absence keeps the call out of `callees_at`, so
the §3a walk cannot ask whether the callee consumes the value and records an
ESCAPE instead: a substantial share of INV-busis's "genuine non-call escape
sites" are calls sitting in exactly this state.

ONLY THE ATTRIBUTE-CHAIN HALF IS FIXED. The builtin-bare-Name half of this item
is deliberately left alone because the two have OPPOSITE directions: chains can
miss a catalogued sink (recall up), while a builtin like `print` mostly turns a
call into an escape site, and emitting it changes anything only where a
TERMINATING summary exists — where it does, it produces more `False`, which
since #214 earns `sanitized` and drops a flow (suppressive). Fixing both as one
change would net two opposite effects and report the sum.

A CLAIM OF MINE, CORRECTED BEFORE IT SHIPPED: this defect does NOT suppress
`builtins.open` sink detection. `open()` emits and matches the catalogued
`builtins.open` host_fs sink — py.py carries an `EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES`
whitelist for it. Measured: open YES; print/len/isinstance/eval/exec/input/
sorted NO. The builtin gap is a small whitelist, not builtins in general.

DIRECTION MEASURED ON BOTH SIGNS, kserve: evidence rows 22 -> 22, sanitized
0 -> 0, violated verdicts 4 -> 4, escape sites 3 -> 3. ZERO CHANGE, against
2,155 new call edges at the analyzer level. Capability moved, adjudication did
not (the WI-nonad shape). No suppression introduced, which is the half that
mattered.

The byte-identical before/after was checked against the stale-read tell rather
than assumed: the cache key includes an analyzer-identity hash that genuinely
differs between the arms (dfb37ba5a9bfc9f9 with the fix, 9fff4c1df3d299e5
without), so both arms analysed fresh. The zero is real.

WHY LAND A MEASURED ZERO: the new edges are logger.info, dict.get, str.replace,
argparse.add_argument — none catalogued sinks. The population that would move is
Django-shaped, and pretix (where this item's four-adjacent-lines measurement came
from) is not on this machine. A false-negative route closed before a repo that
exercises it arrives — the same reasoning as the WI-joluk gate. The size of the
population whose sink calls are ALL chain-shaped remains UNMEASURED; this fix
makes it measurable for the first time, it does not size it.

Tests: 22004 passed, mypy ratchet clean, all six packages 100% in isolation.
